        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Wash it. Sealer is only as well attached as the layer it bonded to, and on a swept-but-unwashed driveway that layer is fine dust. The washing step is what turns a coat that lasts two to three years into one that peels in patches the first spring.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On asphalt, keep the pressure moderate — roughly 1,500 to 2,500 PSI, a 25 or 40 degree fan, about a foot from the surface, wand always moving. Better still, use a rotating surface cleaner, which spreads the force evenly, eliminates wand striping and cuts the job time substantially. Never put a zero-degree tip on asphalt.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then wait. A full 24 dry hours after washing, longer if it is cool or humid, and check the shaded strips and cracks rather than the open middle. Combined with crack filling and its own cure time, this makes sealing a three-day project. Planning for that up front is what separates a job that lasts from one that fails for reasons that had nothing to do with the sealer you chose.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you do not own a pressure washer, renting one for the day alongside a surface cleaner attachment is usually better economics than buying for a job you do every two or three years — and a driveway cleaner worked in with a stiff push broom and rinsed with a hose will get most residential surfaces clean enough.
          </p>
        </div>


